{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "sim-02-brackets-and-virnaculum",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Brackets and the Virnaculum (Bar)",
    "description": "Remove brackets in the correct order — bar first, then ( ), then { }, then [ ] — and handle a minus sign before a bracket.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with two identical-looking lines giving different answers, then build up: one bracket, three nested shapes, the minus-flip, the virnaculum bar, letter expressions, and brackets feeding division. Every worked line changes exactly one layer, and each idea is checked with a fresh question and a parallel retry.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 4, Simplification. Approved scope: Teach that a bracket is a package that must be simplified before anything outside it. Introduce the three bracket shapes ( ), { }, [ ] and the rule to open the innermost first, plus the virnaculum (bar) which is simplified before BODMAS even begins, acting like invisible brackets. Show how a minus sign before a bracket changes the sign of every term inside when the bracket is opened, with both number examples and simple letter expressions in the style of b − [b − (a + b) − {b − (b − a + b)} + 2a]. Keep letter work at one-variable-plus-one level. Include expressions where a bracketed value is then divided or multiplied under BODMAS. Learners should rewrite each line with one bracket layer removed and say what changed."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn how brackets control an expression.\n\nYou will meet three bracket shapes, one invisible bracket called a bar, and the famous minus-sign flip — the exact places where exam options set their traps.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's see how two tiny curved lines change everything.\n\n12 − 5 + 3 = 10\n12 − (5 + 3) = 4\n\nSame numbers, same signs — different answers. The brackets win.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A bracket is a package. Whatever sits inside ( ) must be simplified before anything outside touches it.\n\n12 − (5 + 3)\n= 12 − 8\n= 4\n\nWithout the package, you just go left to right: 12 − 5 + 3 = 7 + 3 = 10.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
